---
/**
 * Lifecycle — homepage section, "Delivery is the first stage of the next bid."
 *
 * Implements the owner-approved Figma frame M7 "Closed loop"
 * (wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU, node 79:53, page 72:2 "Section — Lifecycle v2
 * (market-neutral)"), owner decision 2026-08-02.
 *
 * ── WHAT THE SECTION ARGUES, AND WHY IT IS A RING ────────────────────────────
 *
 * The previous version of this file was a four-tab interface. Tabs present a
 * sequence you step through and then leave. That is the wrong shape for the
 * claim: Deliver is not the end of the process, it is the input to the next
 * bid, because the evidence a contract produces while you deliver it is what
 * makes the next answer credible. A ring closes; a rail stops. The geometry is
 * the argument, which is also why this section makes no competitor claim.
 *
 * ── THE COMPETITOR CLAIMS THAT ARE NOT HERE ──────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Earlier lifecycle drafts (M1, M4) each carried a line asserting that rival
 * tools stop before the post-award half and that hardly anyone competes there.
 * Both are unsourced, sweeping statements about every rival in the market,
 * which is the same class of claim already stripped as OA-18 and OA-01. They
 * are not softened here, they are absent: the drawing makes the point without
 * asserting anything about anyone else.
 *
 * ── WHY THERE IS ALMOST NO TEXT LEFT (owner review, 2026-08-02, second pass) ─
 *
 * "Same for 'One engine, end to end' — give a link into a detailed page and keep
 * less content here", followed by the verdict on the whole page: it reads as a
 * research site rather than a product site.
 *
 * Four things were cut, and the test applied to each was: cover the words with
 * your hand, and does the ring plus the heading still make the point?
 *
 *   the per-stage sentences   Yes. "Find, Answer, Commit, Deliver" arranged in a
 *                             closed ring under the heading "Delivery is the
 *                             first stage of the next bid." IS the argument. The
 *                             sentences restated in prose what the geometry
 *                             already showed, which is the definition of
 *                             commentary.
 *   the per-stage examples    Reference vocabulary. Moved to /sources.
 *   the three market blocks   Reference material, and the largest single block
 *                             of text on the homepage. Moved to /sources whole,
 *                             including the property that matters: the UK row
 *                             keeps s.52 and s.71 as two steps, and the EU row
 *                             keeps Art. 70 as the post-award duty.
 *   the caption and the note  The caption's one load-bearing clause, that arc
 *                             LENGTH is emphasis and not measured duration, is
 *                             not deleted. It moved into the SVG's own <desc>,
 *                             which is where a statement about how a drawing
 *                             should be read actually belongs. The note's s.52 /
 *                             s.71 / National TOMs content is on /sources in
 *                             fuller form than it had here.
 *
 * WHAT MUST NOT COME BACK. A sentence under a stage name. If a future reader
 * cannot tell what "Commit" means from the ring alone, the fix is the ring or
 * the heading, not a line of prose under the label.
 *
 * ── NO FIGURES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * The three unsourced statistics banned under OA-27 (an annual notice count
 * and two percentages) do not appear, in any form. Deliver's arc is drawn long
 * because delivery outlasts the tender, and the caption says in as many words
 * that the arc lengths carry emphasis and are not measured durations. A
 * drawing must never be dressed as data.
 *
 * ── WHY THE STAGE IS CALLED "COMMIT" ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Recorded rather than hidden: Commit is the blandest word on the page and it
 * is the stage that sells the product. It is deliberately not "Prove" or
 * "Evidence". Both of those mean substantiate something already true, and
 * stage three holds forward priced promises: PPN 002 commitments, EU Art. 67
 * and 70 conditions, s.52 KPIs, SLAs. You cannot prove what you have not done
 * yet, and "Evidence" would collide with the post-award artefact name. The
 * specificity therefore rides in the heading and in the stage's own line
 * ("priced before you sign") rather than in the label.
 *
 * ── THE EU POST-AWARD DUTY IS ART. 70 MONITORING ─────────────────────────────
 *
 * Earlier drafts of this file put the US federal contractor-performance
 * reporting system in the EU column, in several places. It is a US system and
 * belongs in neither the EU nor the UK vocabulary; the EU post-award duty is
 * Art. 70 monitoring, and that is what is written. Separately, s.52 (minimum
 * KPIs) and s.71 (assessment against them) are kept as two distinct duties.
 * Merging them was a live defect, OA-26.
 *
 * ── NO JAVASCRIPT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * This component ships zero script. Every word is in the document at first
 * paint. That is deliberate: a staged reveal elsewhere on this site failed the
 * axe gate on seven nodes because correct content depended on an observer
 * firing. The only motion is an ambient pulse on the core glow, gated behind
 * prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference, and nothing reads it for meaning.
 *
 * ── THE DIAGRAM IS SVG, NOT CSS ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * conic-gradient can sweep, but it cannot give each arc its own radius and
 * band width, cannot draw the leader stubs or the closing arrowhead, and
 * cannot carry <title>/<desc>. Every path below is computed in this
 * frontmatter from one polar helper, so the geometry is stated once as angles
 * rather than as hand-typed path strings nobody can check.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

/* ── Geometry ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   All angles are degrees CLOCKWISE FROM 12 O'CLOCK, matching how the frame
   reads. The spans below are measured off the Figma spoke positions, which is
   why they are not round numbers: the spokes sit at 0, 60, 130 and 192.

   Deliver spans 192 to 354, which is 162 degrees, close to half the ring. The
   remaining 6 degrees at the top are the gap the closing arrowhead sits in. */
const CX = 200;
const CY = 200;

/** A point on a circle of radius r at `deg` clockwise from 12 o'clock. */
function polar(r: number, deg: number): [number, number] {
  const a = ((deg - 90) * Math.PI) / 180;
  return [CX + r * Math.cos(a), CY + r * Math.sin(a)];
}
const f = (n: number) => Number(n.toFixed(2));

/** A circular arc, swept clockwise. */
function arcPath(r: number, from: number, to: number): string {
  const [x1, y1] = polar(r, from);
  const [x2, y2] = polar(r, to);
  return `M${f(x1)} ${f(y1)}A${r} ${r} 0 ${to - from > 180 ? 1 : 0} 1 ${f(x2)} ${f(y2)}`;
}

/** A radial line: a spoke, a leader stub or a bracket cap. */
function spokePath(deg: number, r1: number, r2: number): string {
  const [x1, y1] = polar(r1, deg);
  const [x2, y2] = polar(r2, deg);
  return `M${f(x1)} ${f(y1)}L${f(x2)} ${f(y2)}`;
}

/** A triangle sitting on the ring at `deg`, pointing clockwise along it. */
function arrowPoints(deg: number, r: number, len = 10, half = 5.5): string {
  const a = ((deg - 90) * Math.PI) / 180;
  const [px, py] = polar(r, deg);
  const tx = -Math.sin(a);
  const ty = Math.cos(a);
  const nx = Math.cos(a);
  const ny = Math.sin(a);
  const bx = px - len * 0.4 * tx;
  const by = py - len * 0.4 * ty;
  return [
    [px + len * tx, py + len * ty],
    [bx + half * nx, by + half * ny],
    [bx - half * nx, by - half * ny],
  ]
    .map(([x, y]) => `${f(x)},${f(y)}`)
    .join(' ');
}

interface Stage {
  /** Grid area name, and the class suffix. */
  key: 'find' | 'answer' | 'commit' | 'deliver';
  /**
   * ONE WORD. The stage name is the whole label now: no sentence under it, no
   * vocabulary chips beside it. See the header note for the test that was
   * applied and for where the removed text went.
   */
  name: string;
  start: number;
  end: number;
}

const STAGES: Stage[] = [
  { key: 'find', name: 'Find', start: 0, end: 60 },
  { key: 'answer', name: 'Answer', start: 60, end: 130 },
  { key: 'commit', name: 'Commit', start: 130, end: 192 },
  { key: 'deliver', name: 'Deliver', start: 192, end: 354 },
];

/* Band radii and widths. Deliver is drawn wider and reaching further in and
   out than the other three, so it holds the eye without needing a caption to
   say which arc matters. */
const R_STAGE = 132;
const W_STAGE = 30;
const R_DELIVER = 129;
const W_DELIVER = 48;
const R_BRACKET = 163;
const R_CORE = 52;
/** Clear space at each end of an arc, so the joins read as cuts not seams. */
const GAP = 3;

const arcs = STAGES.map((s) => ({
  key: s.key,
  d: arcPath(
    s.key === 'deliver' ? R_DELIVER : R_STAGE,
    s.start + GAP,
    s.end - GAP
  ),
}));

/** Boundary spokes, core out to the inner edge of the ring. */
const spokes = STAGES.map((s) => spokePath(s.start, 58, 98));

/** Leader stubs at each arc's midpoint, pointing outward at its label. */
const leaders = STAGES.map((s) => {
  const mid = (s.start + s.end) / 2;
  return s.key === 'deliver'
    ? spokePath(mid, R_BRACKET + 6, R_BRACKET + 28)
    : spokePath(mid, R_STAGE + W_STAGE / 2 + 3, R_STAGE + W_STAGE / 2 + 20);
});

/* ── THE RING'S HUE, AND IT CLOSES ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Figma `145:2` (M3) states it: "the ring ramp CLOSES: teal at 12 -> cyan at 4
 * -> violet at 8 -> teal at 12. The colour completes the same loop the geometry
 * does. No orchid anywhere on this ring, it is not a refusal."
 *
 * WHY VIOLET IS SAFE HERE, AND THE TEST THAT SAYS SO. The lighting rig on frame
 * `141:2` gives one question: change an element's hue and ask whether the
 * MEANING changed. This section argues that delivery outlasts the tender, and it
 * argues it with ARC LENGTH, the outer bracket and the closing arrow. Recolour
 * any arc and every one of those still says the same thing, so the band colour
 * is light rather than a marker and the full range is open to it.
 *
 * TWO PROPERTIES ARE WHAT KEEP IT LIGHT, AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING:
 *
 *   1. IT IS CONTINUOUS. Each arc takes a linear gradient from the hue at its
 *      own start angle to the hue at its own end angle, so Answer begins on
 *      exactly the hue Find ended on and there is no visible join anywhere on
 *      the ring. Four flat per-arc fills would read as four categories, which
 *      is the specific defect corrected on the hero.
 *   2. IT RETURNS. The ramp arrives back at teal at 12 o'clock, where it
 *      started. A colour that comes back to where it began cannot be labelling
 *      the thing it passed through, and the reader is looking at a loop.
 *
 * ORCHID IS ABSENT AND MUST STAY ABSENT. It is the refusal mark on the
 * reasoning trace two sections down, and nothing on this ring is refused.
 */
const HUE_STOPS = ['var(--c-teal)', 'var(--c-cyan)', 'var(--c-violet)'] as const;

/**
 * The ramp hue at an angle, as a token expression. Three 120-degree segments,
 * teal to cyan to violet and back to teal, so hueAt(0) and hueAt(360) are the
 * same colour by construction rather than by anyone remembering to match them.
 *
 * No hex is produced. Every value is a color-mix of two tokens, so the ring
 * still recolours from tokens.css alone.
 */
function hueAt(deg: number): string {
  const a = ((deg % 360) + 360) % 360;
  const seg = Math.min(2, Math.floor(a / 120));
  const t = (a - seg * 120) / 120;
  const from = HUE_STOPS[seg];
  const to = HUE_STOPS[(seg + 1) % 3];
  if (t <= 0) return from;
  if (t >= 1) return to;
  return `color-mix(in srgb, ${to} ${f(t * 100)}%, ${from})`;
}

/**
 * The two endpoints a linear gradient runs between, in user space. An SVG
 * linearGradient is a straight line, so an arc's gradient is defined by the
 * chord from its start point to its end point and colour varies along that
 * chord rather than along the arc.
 */
function chord(r: number, from: number, to: number) {
  const [x1, y1] = polar(r, from);
  const [x2, y2] = polar(r, to);
  return { x1: f(x1), y1: f(y1), x2: f(x2), y2: f(y2) };
}

/**
 * Where an angle on the arc lands as a 0..1 offset along that chord, by
 * projection. This is what lets a long arc carry a correct multi-stop ramp: the
 * gradient is straight, the arc is not, so each sample angle has to be placed
 * at the offset its own point projects to instead of at an even spacing. Get
 * this wrong and the colour visibly stalls in the middle of the arc.
 */
function projectOnChord(r: number, from: number, to: number, deg: number): number {
  const [ax, ay] = polar(r, from);
  const [bx, by] = polar(r, to);
  const [px, py] = polar(r, deg);
  const dx = bx - ax;
  const dy = by - ay;
  const len2 = dx * dx + dy * dy;
  if (len2 === 0) return 0;
  const t = ((px - ax) * dx + (py - ay) * dy) / len2;
  return Math.min(1, Math.max(0, t));
}

/**
 * A ramp along an arc, as gradient stops. Samples every 20 degrees so no stop
 * is ever more than a fifth of a stage apart, then places each at its projected
 * offset. Consecutive arcs sample their shared boundary angle to the identical
 * expression, which is what makes the joins invisible and the ring one light.
 */
function rampStops(r: number, from: number, to: number, blendWith?: string) {
  const stops: { offset: number; colour: string }[] = [];
  const step = 20;
  for (let a = from; a < to; a += step) {
    stops.push({ offset: projectOnChord(r, from, to, a), colour: hueAt(a) });
  }
  stops.push({ offset: 1, colour: hueAt(to) });
  return stops.map((s) => ({
    offset: f(s.offset),
    // The three narrow stage bands sit at low saturation, mixed into the same
    // surface colour they use today, so Deliver keeps its emphasis. Deliver
    // itself passes no blend and takes the ramp at full strength.
    colour: blendWith ? `color-mix(in srgb, ${s.colour} 34%, ${blendWith})` : s.colour,
  }));
}

/** The bands, with the ramp sampled across each one's own span. */
const bandGradients = STAGES.filter((s) => s.key !== 'deliver').map((s) => ({
  id: `lc-g-${s.key}`,
  ...chord(R_STAGE, s.start + GAP, s.end - GAP),
  stops: rampStops(R_STAGE, s.start + GAP, s.end - GAP, 'var(--c-card-hover)'),
}));

const deliverStage = STAGES.find((s) => s.key === 'deliver')!;
const deliverGradient = {
  id: 'lc-g-deliver',
  ...chord(R_DELIVER, deliverStage.start + GAP, deliverStage.end - GAP),
  stops: rampStops(R_DELIVER, deliverStage.start + GAP, deliverStage.end - GAP),
};

/*
 * ── THE TRAVERSE, AND WHY IT IS AN OVERLAY ────────────────────────────────
 *
 * `145:8`: "The whole ring, all four arcs, the bracket and the closing arrow,
 * complete and legible. A true draw-on would start this empty, and AN EMPTY
 * RING IS A WRONG RING." So the light does not DRAW the ring. It WRITES IT A
 * SECOND TIME: a hot head runs along the finished arc with a trail behind it,
 * over the top of a drawing that was already whole.
 *
 * Two overlay paths, one per leg, because the two legs run at different speeds.
 * The first covers Find, Answer and Commit — 186 degrees in 1,400ms. The second
 * covers Deliver — 156 degrees in the same 1,400ms, half the angular speed.
 * That pacing IS the section's claim: delivery outlasting the tender, made by
 * how long the light takes rather than by a caption saying so.
 *
 * Both take pathLength=1000, so the dash arithmetic in the stylesheet is in
 * thousandths of an arc and is identical for two paths of different real
 * lengths. Nothing has to be measured at runtime.
 */
const traverseStage = {
  id: 'lc-g-trav-stage',
  d: arcPath(R_STAGE, GAP, 192 - GAP),
  ...chord(R_STAGE, GAP, 192 - GAP),
  stops: rampStops(R_STAGE, GAP, 192 - GAP),
};

const traverseDeliver = {
  id: 'lc-g-trav-deliver',
  d: arcPath(R_DELIVER, deliverStage.start + GAP, deliverStage.end - GAP),
  ...chord(R_DELIVER, deliverStage.start + GAP, deliverStage.end - GAP),
  stops: rampStops(R_DELIVER, deliverStage.start + GAP, deliverStage.end - GAP),
};

/** Every gradient the ring needs, each carrying its own id. */
const ringGradients = [
  ...bandGradients,
  deliverGradient,
  traverseStage,
  traverseDeliver,
];

/** The outer duration bracket over Deliver, with a cap at each end. */
const bracket = arcPath(R_BRACKET, 194, 351);
const bracketCaps = [spokePath(194, 157, 169), spokePath(351, 157, 169)];
/** The arrowhead in the top gap: Deliver arriving back at Find. */
const arrow = arrowPoints(356, R_BRACKET);

/*
 * THE MARKET VOCABULARY BLOCKS ARE NOT DEFINED HERE ANY MORE. Three <dl>s, one
 * per market, used to render under the ring and were the largest block of text
 * on the homepage. They now live in src/pages/sources.astro, under
 * /sources#vocabulary, moved verbatim. Do not re-add them here: reference
 * material on a product homepage is what the owner's "research website" verdict
 * was about.
 *
 * The diagram's TEXT EQUIVALENT is therefore the <desc> below plus the four
 * stage names, not those blocks. That is the correct arrangement rather than a
 * concession: a text equivalent describes the drawing, and the drawing encodes
 * four stages, their order, and Deliver's length. It never encoded the vocabulary.
 */
---

<Section
  eyebrow="One engine, end to end"
  title="Delivery is the first stage of the next bid."
  standfirst="Find, Answer, Commit, Deliver. Then Find again."
  id="lifecycle"
>
  {/*
    `data-lit` at threshold 0.4, per `145:3`. The ring is complete at first
    paint; this only decides when a light is allowed to run across it. If
    src/scripts/motion.ts never loads, the attribute stays unset, no keyframe
    matches, and the reader gets the whole finished ring, which is the rest
    frame the board specifies.
  */}
  <div class="lc" data-spot="off" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.4">
    <div class="lc__amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="lc__amb-a"></b>
      <b class="lc__amb-b"></b>
      {/* The cursor light and the grain. Both are mounted inside the ambient
          wrapper, which is z-index -1 in an isolated stacking context, so
          neither can reach the surface a word sits on. <i> rather than <b>, so
          neither joins the shared drift or shifts its phase offsets. */}
      <i class="sv-spot"></i>
      <i class="sv-grain"></i>
    </div>

    <div class="lc__diagram">
      <div class="lc__ring">
        <div class="lc__glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>

        <svg
          class="lc__svg"
          viewBox="0 0 400 400"
          role="img"
          aria-labelledby="lc-svg-title lc-svg-desc"
          style={`--w-stage:${W_STAGE};--w-deliver:${W_DELIVER};--w-halo:${W_DELIVER + 14}`}
        >
          <title id="lc-svg-title">The four stages drawn as a closed ring.</title>
          {/*
            THE DESC CARRIES THE CAPTION'S ONE LOAD-BEARING CLAUSE. A visible
            caption under the ring used to say that the arc lengths are emphasis
            rather than measured durations. That is a statement about how to read
            a drawing, so it belongs in the drawing's own description rather than
            as a fourth paragraph of homepage prose. It is not softened and not
            dropped: a graphic dressed as data is the defect this section was
            rebuilt to avoid.
          */}
          <desc id="lc-svg-desc">
            Find, Answer, Commit and Deliver run clockwise around a single central
            record, and each is named beside the diagram. Deliver is drawn as much the
            longest arc because delivery outlasts every stage before it; the arc
            lengths carry emphasis and are not measured durations. An arrow at the top
            carries Deliver back into Find, so the ring closes rather than stopping.
          </desc>

          {/*
            THE RING'S RAMP, ONE GRADIENT PER ARC, SAMPLED EVERY 20 DEGREES AND
            PROJECTED ONTO EACH ARC'S OWN CHORD. Consecutive arcs sample their
            shared boundary angle through the same hueAt() call, so Answer opens
            on precisely the colour Find closed on and the ring reads as one
            light rather than as four coloured categories. See the note above
            hueAt() for the marker test this passes and why violet is safe here.
          */}
          <defs>
            {
              ringGradients.map((g) => (
                <linearGradient
                  id={g.id}
                  x1={g.x1}
                  y1={g.y1}
                  x2={g.x2}
                  y2={g.y2}
                  gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
                >
                  {g.stops.map((s) => (
                    <stop offset={s.offset} style={`stop-color:${s.colour}`} />
                  ))}
                </linearGradient>
              ))
            }
          </defs>

          {spokes.map((d) => <path class="lc__spoke" d={d} />)}

          {
            arcs
              .filter((a) => a.key !== 'deliver')
              .map((a) => (
                <>
                  <path class="lc__band" d={a.d} stroke={`url(#lc-g-${a.key})`} />
                  <path
                    class="lc__hair"
                    d={arcPath(
                      R_STAGE - W_STAGE / 2,
                      STAGES.find((s) => s.key === a.key)!.start + GAP,
                      STAGES.find((s) => s.key === a.key)!.end - GAP
                    )}
                  />
                  <path
                    class="lc__hair"
                    d={arcPath(
                      R_STAGE + W_STAGE / 2,
                      STAGES.find((s) => s.key === a.key)!.start + GAP,
                      STAGES.find((s) => s.key === a.key)!.end - GAP
                    )}
                  />
                </>
              ))
          }

          <path class="lc__halo" d={arcs.find((a) => a.key === 'deliver')!.d} />
          <path
            class="lc__deliver"
            d={arcs.find((a) => a.key === 'deliver')!.d}
            stroke="url(#lc-g-deliver)"
          />

          {/*
            THE TRAVERSE. Four paths laid OVER a ring that is already whole: a
            trail and a hot head per leg. They are the last thing drawn so they
            sit on top, they are aria-hidden by the parent svg's own labelling,
            and with no animation running every one of them is dashed entirely
            out of view. Remove this whole block and the diagram is unchanged.
          */}
          <path
            class="lc__trail"
            d={traverseStage.d}
            pathLength="1000"
            stroke={`url(#${traverseStage.id})`}
            style={`--w:${W_STAGE}`}
          />
          <path
            class="lc__head"
            d={traverseStage.d}
            pathLength="1000"
            style={`--w:${W_STAGE}`}
          />
          <path
            class="lc__trail lc__trail--deliver"
            d={traverseDeliver.d}
            pathLength="1000"
            stroke={`url(#${traverseDeliver.id})`}
            style={`--w:${W_DELIVER}`}
          />
          <path
            class="lc__head lc__head--deliver"
            d={traverseDeliver.d}
            pathLength="1000"
            style={`--w:${W_DELIVER}`}
          />

          <path class="lc__bracket" d={bracket} />
          {bracketCaps.map((d) => <path class="lc__bracket" d={d} />)}
          <polygon class="lc__arrow" points={arrow} />

          {leaders.map((d, i) => (
            <path
              class:list={['lc__leader', { 'lc__leader--on': STAGES[i].key === 'deliver' }]}
              d={d}
            />
          ))}

          <circle class="lc__core" cx={CX} cy={CY} r={R_CORE} />
        </svg>

        <p class="lc__core-label" aria-hidden="true">One record</p>
      </div>

      {
        STAGES.map((s) => (
          <div class:list={['lc__stage', `lc__stage--${s.key}`]}>
            <h3 class="lc__stage-name">{s.name}</h3>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    {/* One link, and it is the section's only interactive element. */}
    <p class="lc__more">
      <a href="/crowmark">Follow one record</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<style>
  .lc {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* Ambient, contained inside the wrapper so it can never widen the page.
     Two fields in different corners per the lighting rig (`141:2` L03), teal
     high-left and violet low-right, screen-blended so they add light to a
     near-black page instead of painting two grey discs on it. */
  /* The horizontal bleed is exactly one gutter: enough to reach the viewport
     edge instead of drawing a seam one gutter in from it, and never one pixel
     more. A percentage bleed here overflowed the page at 390 and 768. See the
     same note in MarketShape.astro for the arithmetic. */
  .lc__amb {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    /* Scroll-driven parallax throw. Between the hero's 48px and the numbers'
       26px, so no two consecutive fields travel at the same speed and the page
       does not read as one sheet sliding. See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 38px;
    inset-block: -14%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* All four edges are masked. `overflow: hidden` stops the bleed widening
       the page but cuts a 150px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in
       a straight line draws a visible lighter rectangle. One radial mask fades
       every edge at once, so there is no axis for it to miss. See the full note
       in MarketShape.astro, including the mistake of masking only one axis.  */
    /*
     * `closest-side`, NOT `115% 105%`, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CORRECTION.
     *
     * The earlier value was chosen to fade all four edges at once, which it
     * did — but only partly. An ellipse of 115% x 105% has semi-axes of 0.575w
     * and 0.525h, so the middle of the left edge sits at r = 0.5 / 0.575 =
     * 0.87 of the way along a ramp that only reaches transparent at 1.0. About
     * 25% of the field survives there. On a 150px blur whose own falloff is
     * nearly finished, 25% of nearly nothing is nothing, which is why it held.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 raises the orb stops by roughly 1.5x to
     * 1.9x, and 25% of that is a visible vertical line. Measured on the built
     * result at 1440: a lighter RECTANGLE across this section and the four
     * others sharing this recipe, one gutter in from each side — exactly the
     * defect the original note describes and thought it had closed.
     *
     * `closest-side` gives semi-axes of 0.5w and 0.5h, so the ramp reaches
     * transparent EXACTLY at the middle of every edge and past it at every
     * corner. There is no arithmetic left to get wrong, and it stays correct
     * when a section changes shape.
     */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      rgb(0 0 0) 42%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .lc__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  .lc__amb-a {
    width: 560px;
    height: 460px;
    left: -6%;
    top: -14%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  .lc__amb-b {
    width: 520px;
    height: 440px;
    right: -6%;
    bottom: -16%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .lc__amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 300px; }
  }

  /* ── The radial layout ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Named areas, so the DOM can read Find, Answer, Commit, Deliver in order
     while Deliver is placed opposite the other three. That matters: Deliver
     holds the whole left half of the ring, so its label belongs on the left,
     and no amount of source-order shuffling should be needed to get it there. */
  .lc__diagram {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(260px, 430px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas:
      'deliver ring find'
      'deliver ring answer'
      'deliver ring commit';
    gap: clamp(14px, 2.2vw, 34px);
    align-items: center;
  }

  .lc__ring {
    grid-area: ring;
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    align-self: center;
  }

  .lc__glow {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 27%;
    background: var(--orb-teal);
    opacity: 0.55;
    border-radius: 50%;
    pointer-events: none;
    /*
     * FILTER, NOT OPACITY, AND THAT IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN. `lc-breathe`
     * owns `opacity` on this element for the whole twelve-second cycle, and a
     * running animation beats a transition on the same property — a hover that
     * wrote opacity here would simply never be seen. Brightness is free.
     */
    transition: filter var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  /*
   * THE RING NOW CASTS. Lever 03 in specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md: the drawings on
   * this page are wireframes rather than objects, and the cheapest honest fix
   * on a drawing that must not be redrawn is a shadow underneath it. A drop
   * shadow on an SVG shape follows the shape's own alpha rather than its box,
   * so the long Deliver arc and the central record each cast their own edge —
   * which is what separates this from a rectangle with a shadow behind it.
   *
   * IT IS ON TWO SHAPES AND DELIBERATELY NOT ON THE <svg>. Filtering the root
   * would put every frame of the traverse's dash animation through the filter,
   * so a blur that never changes would be re-rasterised twenty times a second
   * for twelve seconds out of every twelve. These two paths are static, the
   * animated overlays are separate elements, and the visual result is the same.
   *
   * Composed from --c-floor rather than typed as a black, so it is the page's
   * own deepest step and moves with the ladder.
   */
  .lc__deliver,
  .lc__core {
    filter: drop-shadow(0 14px 22px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-floor) 88%, transparent));
    transition: filter var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  /* Ambient only. Nothing is revealed by it and nothing is read from it, so
     it is safe to run always and correct to stop under reduced motion.

     THE BREATH IS NOW --m-drift, NOT 6s. It was the only ambient period on the
     homepage that was neither 18s nor anything else in particular, and an
     ambient layer running at three different speeds is what the grammar block
     in styles/tokens.css exists to stop. Same idea, one period, shared with the
     two blurred fields behind this section and the three inside the closing
     card. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .lc__glow {
      animation: lc-breathe var(--m-drift) var(--m-ease-breath) infinite;
    }

    /*
     * ONE BEAT AS THE ARROW LANDS, ON THE SAME CLOCK AS THE ARROW, so the glow
     * and the arrowhead are one event repeated once per cycle rather than two
     * animations that used to happen to coincide at 2,900ms.
     *
     * IT IS A SCALE, NOT AN OPACITY, AND THAT IS THE FIX. Both this and the
     * breath used to write `opacity`, which was survivable when the beat ran
     * once for 600ms and is not survivable now that both loop: an animation
     * declared later owns a property for its WHOLE duration, so a cycle-length
     * beat on opacity would take the property for the entire twelve seconds and
     * the breath would never be seen again. Splitting them by property is what
     * lets both loop. It also only ever adds light, which is rule 02.
     */
    [data-lit='on'] .lc__glow {
      animation:
        lc-breathe var(--m-drift) var(--m-ease-breath) infinite,
        lc-beat var(--m-cycle) var(--m-ease-land) infinite;
    }
  }

  @keyframes lc-breathe {
    0%, 100% { opacity: 0.42; }
    50%      { opacity: 0.68; }
  }

  /* The arrow lands at 8 beats, which is 40% of the cycle. */
  @keyframes lc-beat {
    0%, 38%     { transform: scale(1); }
    41%         { transform: scale(1.12); }
    45%, 100%   { transform: scale(1); }
  }

  .lc__svg {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* Every stroke width comes from the frontmatter constants via custom
     properties on the <svg>, so band width and arc radius can never drift
     apart in two files.

     The stroke is a per-arc gradient set on the element; the flat
     --c-card-hover it used to carry is now the colour the ramp is MIXED INTO
     at 34%, so the bands sit at the same low saturation they always did and
     Deliver keeps its emphasis. See rampStops() in the frontmatter. */
  .lc__band {
    fill: none;
    stroke-width: var(--w-stage);
  }

  .lc__hair {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--c-border);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  .lc__halo {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--c-border-glow);
    stroke-width: var(--w-halo);
    opacity: 0.28;
  }

  /* Deliver takes the ramp at full strength across its own 156 degrees, which
     runs it from the violet end of the ring back up to teal at 12 o'clock,
     closing the loop where it started. Orchid is absent from this ring and must
     stay absent: it is the refusal mark two sections down and nothing here is
     refused. See the note above hueAt() for why this is light, not a label. */
  .lc__deliver {
    fill: none;
    stroke-width: var(--w-deliver);
  }

  .lc__bracket,
  .lc__leader,
  .lc__spoke {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--c-border);
    stroke-width: 1.25;
  }

  .lc__bracket {
    stroke: var(--c-teal);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .lc__leader--on {
    stroke: var(--c-teal);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .lc__arrow {
    fill: var(--c-teal);
  }

  /* ══ M3 · THE LOOP CLOSING ════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `145:2` — "The section whose whole argument is a loop, closing".
   * TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.4.
   * CADENCE 8 beats: four for the first leg, four for Deliver, on --m-cycle.
   * PLAYS: LOOPS. Twelve beats of rest, then the loop closes again.
   * REDUCED MOTION complete static ring, no traverse, no beat.
   *
   * OF EVERY SECTION ON THIS PAGE THIS IS THE ONE THAT MOST WANTED TO LOOP, and
   * it is worth saying why the owner's instruction lands well here rather than
   * merely being obeyed. The section's entire claim is that delivery feeds the
   * next bid: a ring that closes and then stops argues against itself, and a
   * ring that closes, rests, and closes again is the thing the drawing says.
   *
   * THE PACING ARGUMENT IS UNTOUCHED. The first leg covers 186 degrees in four
   * beats and Deliver covers 156 in the same four, so the light still visibly
   * slows on the long arc. That is the section's argument made as pacing rather
   * than as a caption, and it survived the retiming because both legs were
   * scaled by the same factor.
   *
   * THE RING IS NEVER DRAWN FROM EMPTY. This is the point the board makes
   * hardest and the one thing to check if anyone edits this block: a true
   * draw-on starts the ring missing, and a missing ring is a WRONG ring, not a
   * ring in progress. Every arc, the bracket and the closing arrow are painted
   * at 100% before any of this exists. The light writes the ring a SECOND time
   * over the top of the finished one, which reads as drawing and is never
   * incomplete.
   *
   * The dash arithmetic is in thousandths, because both overlay paths declare
   * pathLength="1000". A 30-unit head with a 200-unit trail behind it: at rest
   * both are dashed entirely off the path, so the overlays occupy no pixels
   * until something starts them.
   *
   * THE TRAIL IS A CONSTANT-LENGTH BAND, NOT A PER-POINT DECAY. The board
   * specifies each point on the arc lifting and decaying over 900ms behind the
   * head. That is a per-point timeline, which SVG dashes cannot express; a
   * 200-unit trail travelling at the head's speed means a given point stays lit
   * for about 280ms on the first leg and 560ms on Deliver. Same effect, coarser
   * decay, and honest about it. It never drops below the ring's rest brightness
   * because it is drawn OVER a ring already at full strength.
   */
  .lc__head,
  .lc__trail {
    fill: none;
    stroke-linecap: butt;
    /* Two thirds of the band's width, so the light sits inside the arc it is
       writing rather than spilling over both hairlines. */
    stroke-width: calc(var(--w) * 0.66);
    /* Dashed completely out of view. This is the rest state and it is what
       ships when nothing runs. */
    stroke-dasharray: 0 1000;
  }

  /* WHITE CORE. `141:2` L04: a lamp has a hot white centre and a marker is a
     flat coloured stroke. The trail beneath carries the hue, sampled from the
     ring's own ramp at whatever angle the light currently occupies, because the
     gradient is fixed in user space and the dash moves through it. */
  .lc__head {
    stroke: var(--c-text);
  }

  .lc__trail {
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    [data-lit='on'] .lc__head {
      stroke-dasharray: 30 1000;
      animation: lc-head var(--m-cycle) var(--m-ease-travel) infinite;
    }

    [data-lit='on'] .lc__trail {
      stroke-dasharray: 200 1000;
      animation: lc-trail var(--m-cycle) var(--m-ease-travel) infinite;
    }

    /* THE SECOND LEG IS THE SECTION'S ARGUMENT, MADE AS PACING. Deliver's 156
       degrees take the same four beats the first leg's 186 took, so the light
       visibly slows. Delivery outlasting the tender, said by how long the eye
       waits rather than by a caption. */
    [data-lit='on'] .lc__head--deliver,
    [data-lit='on'] .lc__trail--deliver {
      animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 4);
    }

    /* The arrowhead closes the loop: a nudge along the ring into Find and a
       glow, out and back so the finished drawing is byte-identical to the rest
       state. Overshoot on the curve, because a loop shutting is a mechanism —
       the one place on the page that is deliberately not one of the grammar's
       three curves, recorded here rather than left to be found. */
    [data-lit='on'] .lc__arrow {
      animation: lc-arrow var(--m-cycle) cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) infinite;
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── THE DRAIN, AND IT IS A BUG FIX RATHER THAN A RETIMING ─────────────────
   *
   * The head and the trail are locked 170 units apart: the trail is 200 long
   * and the head 30, so `trail offset = head offset + 170` puts the trail's
   * LEADING edge exactly on the head with its 200 units extending backwards
   * along the path behind it. That relationship holds at every keyframe below
   * and is the only thing keeping them one object.
   *
   * WHAT WAS WRONG. Both animations used to be `forwards` and both used to stop
   * the instant the head left the path — head at -1000, trail at -830. A dash
   * pattern starting at 830 on a path of length 1000 is still 170 units ON that
   * path, so the trail's tail was left permanently lit over the last sixth of
   * each leg. At opacity 0.75 over an arc already drawn at full strength that
   * was easy to miss when it happened once. It is not missable at four times a
   * minute, and it would have read as the light snagging.
   *
   * So there is now a drain: three per cent of the cycle in which the head runs
   * on to -1,170 and the trail to -1,000, at which point both dash patterns
   * begin past the end of the path and neither occupies a pixel. The travel
   * itself still ends at 20%; the drain is after it and costs the pacing
   * nothing.
   */
  @keyframes lc-head {
    0%        { stroke-dashoffset: 30; }
    20%       { stroke-dashoffset: -1000; }
    23%       { stroke-dashoffset: -1170; }
    100%      { stroke-dashoffset: -1170; }
  }

  @keyframes lc-trail {
    0%        { stroke-dashoffset: 200; }
    20%       { stroke-dashoffset: -830; }
    23%       { stroke-dashoffset: -1000; }
    100%      { stroke-dashoffset: -1000; }
  }

  /* At 12 o'clock, clockwise along the ring is straight to the right, so the
     nudge into Find is +x. Filter rather than opacity: the arrow is already at
     full opacity at rest and rule 02 forbids taking it down to animate it up.
     The landing is at 8 beats, which is 40% of the cycle. */
  @keyframes lc-arrow {
    0%, 38%   { transform: translateX(0); filter: none; }
    40%  {
      transform: translateX(6px);
      filter: drop-shadow(0 0 8px var(--c-teal-glow));
    }
    42%, 100% { transform: translateX(0); filter: none; }
  }

  /* THE CORE IS THE THIRD SURFACE STEP. It was --c-card, a 55%-alpha fill that
     showed the teal glow directly behind it straight through and so read as a
     hole in the ring rather than as the one record the ring turns around. It is
     opaque now, which is what makes it an object. Same value the raised panels
     elsewhere on the page take, so the ladder is one ladder. */
  .lc__core {
    fill: var(--c-raised);
    stroke: var(--c-border);
    stroke-width: 1;
    transition:
      fill var(--dur) var(--ease),
      stroke var(--dur) var(--ease),
      filter var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .lc__core-label {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: grid;
    place-content: center;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 9ch;
    margin-inline: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: center;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-teal);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* ── Stage labels ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/
  /* ══ THE STAGE LABELS ANSWER A CURSOR ══════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * This section had ZERO :hover rules — one of the four
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6 names, and the reason the whole page
   * measured 2.7% of elements carrying a transition against a comparator's
   * 16.2%.
   *
   * A stage becomes a raised chip under the pointer: the fourth surface step
   * arrives behind it, the hairline draws itself in, and the name comes up to
   * full white. THE PADDING AND THE TRANSPARENT BORDER ARE ON THE REST STATE,
   * not on the hover, so nothing reflows and no label moves by a pixel when the
   * pointer arrives — the only thing that changes is what is painted.
   *
   * Both endpoints clear the contrast floor: --c-text on --c-lit is about 19:1.
   * "Deliver" keeps its teal, which is the one label on the ring carrying a
   * meaning, and teal on --c-lit is 6.7:1 against a 4.5:1 requirement.
   *
   * NOTHING TRANSLATES. A stage name is a label on a diagram, not a control.
   */
  .lc__stage {
    padding: 0.45rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    transition:
      background-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      border-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      box-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .lc__stage-name {
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease), text-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .lc__stage:hover {
      background-color: var(--c-lit);
      border-color: var(--c-border);
      box-shadow: var(--gloss-inset), var(--shadow-ladder);
    }

    .lc__stage:hover .lc__stage-name {
      text-shadow: 0 0 20px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
    }

    /* The ring itself: the core glow opens and the whole drawing throws a
       deeper cast, so the object reads as coming forward without any part of it
       actually moving. */
    .lc__ring:hover .lc__glow { filter: brightness(1.5); }

    .lc__ring:hover .lc__deliver,
    .lc__ring:hover .lc__core {
      filter: drop-shadow(0 18px 30px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-floor) 94%, transparent))
        drop-shadow(0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-teal) 24%, transparent));
    }

    .lc__ring:hover .lc__core { stroke: var(--c-border-glow); }

    .lc__more a:hover { color: var(--c-text); }
  }

  .lc__stage--find { grid-area: find; }
  .lc__stage--answer { grid-area: answer; }
  .lc__stage--commit { grid-area: commit; }
  .lc__stage--deliver {
    grid-area: deliver;
    text-align: right;
  }

  /*
   * THE STAGE NAME IS THE WHOLE LABEL, so it takes --t-h3 rather than the
   * --t-h4 it had when a sentence and a vocabulary row sat underneath it. One
   * word at card-heading size beside a 400px ring reads as an afterthought; at
   * section-subheading size it reads as a station on the diagram, which is what
   * it is. The heading LEVEL is unchanged: size and structure are separate
   * questions, and h3 is still the correct level under the section's h2.
   */
  .lc__stage-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: var(--t-w-h3);
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .lc__stage--deliver .lc__stage-name { color: var(--c-teal); }

  /* ── The one link ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The caption, the three market blocks and the closing note that used to sit
     here are gone: see the header note for the test each failed and for where
     each landed. Their CSS is deleted with them rather than left behind, so a
     future edit cannot quietly reattach a `.lc__market` and inherit a recipe
     nothing renders. */
  .lc__more {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* Cyan is the interactive colour on this site and this is the only
     interactive element in the section, so it is the only cyan here.

     inline-flex + --btn-h-sm, not a bare inline link. Measured at 390, 768 and
     1440 this rendered 21px tall, which fails WCAG 2.5.8: the inline exception
     covers a link inside a sentence, and this is a standalone action alone in
     its own centred row. It was already 21px before the text was shortened; the
     cut only made it obvious. --btn-h-sm IS the 44px floor. */
  .lc__more a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    color: var(--c-cyan);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .lc__more a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* ── The linear fallback ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A radial layout needs room on both sides of the ring for labels, and
     below roughly 900px there is none. Rather than shrink four label blocks
     into unreadable columns, the SAME markup relayouts: the ring becomes a
     capped graphic at the top and the four stages stack beneath it in reading
     order. Nothing is hidden and nothing is duplicated, because the labels
     were never inside the SVG. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .lc__diagram {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      grid-template-areas:
        'ring ring'
        'find answer'
        'commit deliver';
      gap: clamp(20px, 4vw, 32px);
    }

    .lc__ring {
      justify-self: center;
      width: min(100%, 340px);
      margin-bottom: clamp(8px, 2vw, 20px);
    }

    .lc__stage--deliver { text-align: left; }
  }

  /* 390px: two label columns stop working before the ring does, so the ring
     stays and the stages go to one column. At this width the ring renders at
     about 300px, which still reads as a ring; the caption and the market
     blocks carry every word regardless. */
  @media (max-width: 599px) {
    .lc__diagram {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-areas:
        'ring'
        'find'
        'answer'
        'commit'
        'deliver';
    }
  }
</style>
